Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-11-04T15:49Z Minh Ha-Duong created
2026-11-04T16:28Z Minh-Ha-Duong redesigned with the author: doc + injunction + no guard + test-doctor throwaway-worktree probe + sweep (was: harden yawn's probe)

--- body ---

## Context

Yawn's probe (`scripts/project-state.py --tests`, `test_state()`) runs the
project's `make check-fast|check|test` target in the project's primary
checkout. Yawn's SKILL.md calls itself read-only; that holds only when the
project's test target leaves the tree as it found it.

Observed 2026-11-04 in VN-Power-Scenario (2018 research repo, not
harness-shaped): `test: cleaner`, `cleaner: clean`, and `clean` does `rm -f`
on every generated table and figure. A plain `/yawn` deleted 19 untracked
build outputs in the author's checkout. Regenerable with `make all` once the
environment worked; any copy differing from current code was lost. The same
Makefile's `%.txt` rule also runs `sed -i` on tracked `init.py`.

## Decision (author, 2026-11-04)

Fix at the source, not with a guard on yawn. Five parts:

1. **Documentation.** Yawn's SKILL.md stops claiming unconditional
   read-only: the test check executes the project's test target in the
   checkout and is read-only only if that target is.
2. **Injunction.** A project-facing rule: a project's test target
   (`check-fast`, `check`, `test`) leaves the working tree as it found it:
   it deletes, rewrites or creates nothing outside ignored caches.
3. **No guard.** `project-state.py` keeps running the target in place; no
   recipe parsing, no blocking check (rules/workflow.md: prefer deleting a
   guard to growing one; test-doctor owns test-hygiene diagnostics).
4. **test-doctor probe.** Occasionally, per project: make a throwaway
   detached worktree of HEAD, plant a sentinel untracked file, snapshot
   (`git status --porcelain --ignored` plus sentinel hash), run the test
   target, compare. A diff is a diagnostic finding (report, and a ticket in
   that project's store when it has one), never a block. The worktree cost is
   paid at doctor cadence, not on every yawn.
5. **Sweep.** Run the probe once across the author's projects now; each
   offender gets a ticket in its own store (cross-project rule: ticket there,
   never edit it from here). Projects without a store are reported to the
   author.

Rejected: running yawn's tests in a throwaway worktree every time (env setup
cost on every yawn, e.g. a full uv sync); test only declared targets (loses
the signal on legacy repos); `make -n` keyword heuristic (misses other
destruction shapes).

Residual risk accepted: an unswept or new project can still be hit between
probe runs; typical damage is regenerable outputs.

## First sweep case

VN-Power-Scenario (no ticket store; report to author): drop `cleaner` from
the `test` prerequisites, and move the `sed -i ... init.py` out of the
`%.txt` rule (VERBOSE is already False on master).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Yawn's SKILL.md states the test check's read-only condition.
- [ ] The test-target injunction is in the rule set where project-facing
      rules live, one sentence, with its reason.
- [ ] test-doctor carries the throwaway-worktree probe, with a test: a
      fixture repo whose `test` target deletes an untracked file is flagged;
      a clean target is not; the primary checkout is untouched by the probe.
- [ ] The probe never blocks a merge or a yawn (diagnostic only).
- [ ] Sweep run across the author's projects; offenders ticketed in their
      own stores or listed for the author; VN-Power-Scenario reported.
